f.app).\n- Fed Funds Target: 3.50%-3.75%, fifth consecutive hold at July 2026 FOMC [Actual] (TradingEconomics; FRED).\n- Fed Stance: Neutral-to-Hawkish; market assigned ~1/3 probability to a hike [Estimate].\n- Real rates: Positive; 10Y ~4.7% vs core inflation likely ~3% [Estimate].\n- DXY: Not retrievable within research budget [Unconfirmed].\n- Credit spreads: Not retrievable within research budget [Unconfirmed].\n- Economic cycle: Late expansion / potential late-cycle slowdown [Estimate].\n\n1-2. Regime Probability Distribution\n| Regime | Probability |\n|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 20% |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 35% |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 25% |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 20% |\n| Total | 100% |\n\n1-3. Macro Sensitivity (scale -3 to +3)\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -1 (cash-rich, defensive but small-cap)\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (USD revenue base, ILS cost base; mixed)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: -1 (small-cap, but net cash)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -1 (specialty pharma is defensive)\n- Commodity sensitivity: 0\n\n1-4. Macro Alignment\nExpected Macro Impact = 0.20*(+1) + 0.35*(0) + 0.25*(-1) + 0.20*(-2) = -0.45\nJudgment: MILD HEADWIND. Conviction adjustment: -0.5 step.\nMacro does not change intrinsic value; it lowers position size and encourages staged entry.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERNS\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1.5-1. Base Rates\n- Commercial-stage small-cap specialty pharma with positive net income, net cash and FDA-approved products: 5-year survival probability ~85% [Estimate].\n- Probability of growing revenue >10% annually over the next 5 years for a company with current 12% growth: ~35-50% [Estimate].\n- Orphan/niche plasma product franchises maintaining pricing in the face of large competitors: ~50% [Estimate].\n- Large M&A (>$10B) achieving ROIC > WACC: ~30% [General base rate] \u2014 not a current KMDA catalyst.\n\n1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate\nOur base-case success probability of ~75% is slightly below the survival base rate (85%) but above the growth base rate. Justifications:\n1. KMDA has six FDA-approved products, a recurring distribution platform, and a 30-country network.\n2. Net cash ~$53M and positive OCF eliminates financing risk.\n3. Reaffirmed 2026 guidance and first-half results already cover ~50% of targets.\nDeviation is reasonable \u2014 not a red flag.\n\n1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check\nSuccess probability 75% vs base rate ~60% for growth: ratio 1.25x, below the 1.5x escalation threshold. No mandatory re-evaluation.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2-1. Business Understanding\nKamada develops, manufactures and sells plasma-derived specialty therapeutics for rare/serious conditions (KEDRAB rabies, VARIZIG, HEPAGAM, CYTOGAM and others), distributes third-party products in Israel/MENA, and operates plasma collection centers in Texas. Understandability: HIGH.\n\n2-2. Moat Assessment\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) |\n|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (FDA approvals, product rights) | 2 |\n| Switching costs | 2 |\n| Network effects | 0 |\n| Cost advantage | 1 |\n| Economies of scale | 2 |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 |\n| Data moat | 0 |\n| **Total** | **10** |\n\nMoat verdict: NARROW MOAT. Direction: STABLE / slightly NARROWING \u2014 larger plasma players, generic/biosimilar entrants, and gross-margin mix shift are watch items.\n\n2-3. Lynch Classification\nSTALWART with FAST-GROWER characteristics. Revenue CAGR 5-yr +6.2%, recent 12% growth, EPS growth high-teens. Key Lynch checkpoint: verify earnings growth is cash-backed \u2014 partly confirmed by OCF > net income.\n\n2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n| Metric | FY2025 [Actual] |\n|---|---|\n| Revenue growth (2025) | +12.0% |\n| 3-yr Revenue CAGR (2022-25) | +11.8% [Arithmetic] |\n| Operating margin | 14.5% |\n| Net margin | 11.1% |\n| ROE | 8.0% [Aggregator] |\n| FCF / Net income | ~90% after est. capex [Estimate] |\n| Net debt / EBITDA | Net cash ~$53M [Estimate] |\n| Interest coverage | Very high (no material debt) |\n\nVerdict: GOOD to EXCELLENT.\n\n2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard\n| Item | Score (0-3) |\n|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 0 (share count +4.8% 3-yr CAGR; no buyback) |\n| M&A track record | 1 (acquisition history not fully audited) |\n| Capex efficiency | 2 (plasma centers should add $8-10M+ each at ramp) |\n| Rationality of mix | 2 (selective organic + dividend) |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3 (H1 2026 aligned with guidance) |\n| Insider bonus | +1 (7.2% insider ownership) |\n| **Total** | **9/15 + bonus = 10/18 equivalent** |\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2.5-1. R&D Capitalization\nR&D expense is not split in the pack [Unconfirmed]. It is likely $5-$8M/year [Assumption]. Capitalizing R&D would lift Adjusted ROIC modestly, but we do not include it in the base valuation.\n\n2.5-2. SBC Treatment\nFY2025 SBC was $1M [Actual]. This is already deducted from GAAP net income; we treat it as a cash expense in FCF. Impact is immaterial.\n\n2.5-3. Operating Leases\nFY2025 operating leases $12M [Actual]. We add this to net debt in the EV bridge.\n\n2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets\nPlasma product franchise value is captured indirectly through higher EBITDA margins. No separate data-asset value is added. [Assumption]\n\n2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\nGAAP ROIC estimate: NOPAT $20M / invested capital ~$220M = ~9% [Estimate].\nAdjusted EBITDA-based ROIC: ~$42M / ~$220M = ~19% [Estimate].\nThe 10pp gap is due to intangible amortization. We treat amortization as a real cost in valuation.\n\n2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett Style)\nFY2025: NI $20M + D&A $2M - maintenance capex $4M - \u0394WC $11M = $7M [Estimate].\nIf working capital normalizes to 2% of revenue, Owner Earnings ~$15M [Assumption].\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \